- Your approximate credit score (good to excellent helps for balance-transfer/personal-loan offers).
- Do you want to avoid using your home as collateral?

Main principles
- Consolidate only if the new interest rate (after fees) is meaningfully lower than the weighted average APR of your cards and the monthly payment is affordable.
- Beware converting unsecured credit-card debt into secured debt (HELOC/home equity loan) — lower rate but your home becomes collateral.
- Keep an emergency fund (commonly 3–6 months of essential expenses). With $1,600/month rent, 3 months of rent alone is $4,800; factor in utilities, food, insurance, etc.
- Watch out for balance-transfer intro periods that revert to a high APR after the promo; know the length of the intro and the post-intro rate and any transfer fees.
- Prioritize highest-rate debt if you can’t fully consolidate — the avalanche method minimizes interest paid.

Concrete illustrative example
(So you can see how much you might save — I’ll run exact numbers when you give your balances/APRs.)

Example scenario:
- Credit card debt = $20,000 at 20% APR.
- Option A: leave it and pay $400/month on cards.
  - At 20% APR, $400/month would take roughly 9+ years (~109 months) to pay off and cost roughly $23k in interest (very approximate).
- Option B: take a 3-year personal loan at 10% APR for $20,000.
  - Monthly payment ≈ $646 — total paid ≈ $23,263; interest ≈ $3,263.
- Comparison: consolidation to the 10% loan reduces total interest dramatically vs making only $400/month on high-rate cards.

So consolidation can be very beneficial if you can get a significantly lower fixed rate and commit to consistent payments.

Options to consider
- Balance-transfer credit card (0% intro APR for 12–21 months): good if you can pay the balance before the promo ends and the transfer fee (often 3%) is small relative to interest saved.
- Unsecured personal loan: fixed rate, predictable term and payment — often better for budgeting and may lower interest if your credit is good.
- HELOC/home equity loan: often lower rates but secured by your home — only use if you’re comfortable using your house as collateral and understand variable-rate risk and closing fees.
- Pay down from savings: since you have $56k, you could pay much of the debt now — but keep an emergency fund (3–6 months of expenses). Paying from savings eliminates interest but reduces liquidity.
- Negotiate with creditors: call card issuers to ask for a lower rate — sometimes they’ll reduce it if you threaten to leave or transfer balance.

